Dear Elmer,

This seems to be a locale problem.  I don't know much about locale 
problems, but my understanding is that they arise from using an extended 
ascii character which is understood in one language, but not supported by 
the software you're using.  My guess is that your Windows R is compiled 
for a Spanish locale whereas your Unix version is compiled for US English. 
I'm guessing that your data file or targets file contain some extended 
ascii characters not valid in English.

You might be able to solve the problem by using versions of R compiled for 
your locale. It's hard for me to say more, because it's not really an 
issue with limma, and there isn't any way that I could reproduce your 
problem to examine it.

Best wishes
Gordon

